#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-push(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:7
msgid "git-push - Update remote refs along with associated objects"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:18
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"'git push' [--all | --branches | --mirror | --tags] [--follow-tags] "
"[--atomic] [-n | --dry-run] [--receive-pack=<git-receive-pack>]\n"
"\t   [--repo=<repository>] [-f | --force] [-d | --delete] [--prune] [-q | "
"--quiet] [-v | --verbose]\n"
"\t   [-u | --set-upstream] [-o <string> | --push-option=<string>]\n"
"\t   [--[no-]signed|--signed=(true|false|if-asked)]\n"
"\t   [--force-with-lease[=<refname>[:<expect>]] [--force-if-includes]]\n"
"\t   [--no-verify] [<repository> [<refspec>...]]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:25
msgid ""
"Updates one or more branches, tags, or other references in a remote "
"repository from your local repository, and sends all necessary data that "
"isn't already on the remote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:29
msgid ""
"The simplest way to push is `git push <remote> <branch>`.  `git push origin "
"main` will push the local `main` branch to the `main` branch on the remote "
"named `origin`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:32
msgid ""
"The `<repository>` argument defaults to the upstream for the current branch, "
"or `origin` if there's no configured upstream."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:35
msgid ""
"To decide which branches, tags, or other refs to push, Git uses (in order of "
"precedence):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:38
msgid ""
"The `<refspec>` argument(s) (for example `main` in `git push origin main`)  "
"or the `--all`, `--mirror`, or `--tags` options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:39
msgid "The `remote.*.push` configuration for the repository being pushed to"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:42
msgid ""
"The `push.default` configuration. The default is `push.default=simple`, "
"which will push to a branch with the same name as the current branch.  See "
"the <<CONFIGURATION,CONFIGURATION>> section below for more on "
"`push.default`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:47
msgid ""
"`git push` may fail if you haven't set an upstream for the current branch, "
"depending on what `push.default` is set to.  See the "
"<<UPSTREAM-BRANCHES,UPSTREAM BRANCHES>> section below for more on how to set "
"and use upstreams."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:51
msgid ""
"You can make interesting things happen to a repository every time you push "
"into it, by setting up 'hooks' there.  See documentation for "
"linkgit:git-receive-pack[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS[[OPTIONS]]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "<repository>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:60
msgid ""
"The \"remote\" repository that is the destination of a push operation.  This "
"parameter can be either a URL (see the section <<URLS,GIT URLS>> below) or "
"the name of a remote (see the section <<REMOTES,REMOTES>> below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "<refspec>..."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:63
msgid "Specify what destination ref to update with what source object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:66
msgid ""
"The format for a refspec is [+]<src>[:<dst>], for example `main`, "
"`main:other`, or `HEAD^:refs/heads/main`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:69
msgid ""
"The `<src>` is often the name of the local branch to push, but it can be any "
"arbitrary \"SHA-1 expression\" (see linkgit:gitrevisions[7])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:72
msgid ""
"The `<dst>` determines what ref to update on the remote side. It must be the "
"name of a branch, tag, or other ref, not an arbitrary expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:74
msgid "The `+` is optional and does the same thing as `--force`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:80
msgid ""
"You can write a refspec using the fully expanded form (for example "
"`refs/heads/main:refs/heads/main`) which specifies the exact source and "
"destination, or with a shorter form (for example `main` or "
"`main:other`). Here are the rules for how refspecs are expanded, as well as "
"various other special refspec forms:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:85
msgid ""
"`<src>` without a `:<dst>` means to update the same ref as the `<src>`, "
"unless the `remote.<repository>.push` configuration specifies a different "
"<dst>. For example, if `main` is a branch, then the refspec `main` expands "
"to `main:refs/heads/main`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:88
msgid ""
"If `<dst>` unambiguously refers to a ref on the <repository> remote, then "
"expand it to that ref. For example, if `v1.0` is a tag on the remote, then "
"`HEAD:v1.0` expands to `HEAD:refs/tags/v1.0`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:91
msgid ""
"If `<src>` resolves to a ref starting with `refs/heads/` or `refs/tags/`, "
"then prepend that to <dst>. For example, if `main` is a branch, then "
"`main:other` expands to `main:refs/heads/other`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:95
msgid ""
"The special refspec `:` (or `+:` to allow non-fast-forward updates)  directs "
"Git to push \"matching\" branches: for every branch that exists on the local "
"side, the remote side is updated if a branch of the same name already exists "
"on the remote side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:101
msgid ""
"<src> may contain a * to indicate a simple pattern match.  This works like a "
"glob that matches any ref matching the pattern.  There must be only one * in "
"both the `<src>` and `<dst>`.  It will map refs to the destination by "
"replacing the * with the contents matched from the source. For example, "
"`refs/heads/*:refs/heads/*` will push all branches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:109
msgid ""
"A refspec starting with `^` is a negative refspec.  This specifies refs to "
"exclude. A ref will be considered to match if it matches at least one "
"positive refspec, and does not match any negative refspec. Negative refspecs "
"can be pattern refspecs.  They must only contain a `<src>`.  Fully spelled "
"out hex object names are also not supported.  For example, `git push origin "
"'refs/heads/*' '^refs/heads/dev-*'` will push all branches except for those "
"starting with `dev-`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:112
msgid ""
"If `<src>` is empty, it deletes the `<dst>` ref from the remote "
"repository. For example, `git push origin :dev` will delete the `dev` "
"branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:113
msgid "`tag <tag>` expands to `refs/tags/<tag>:refs/tags/<tag>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:116
msgid ""
"This is technically a special syntax for `git push` and not a refspec, since "
"in `git push origin tag v1.0` the arguments `tag` and `v1.0` are separate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:121
msgid ""
"If the refspec can't be expanded unambiguously, error out with an error "
"indicating what was tried, and depending on the "
"`advice.pushUnqualifiedRefname` configuration (see linkgit:git-config[1]) "
"suggest what refs/ namespace you may have wanted to push to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:123
msgid "Not all updates are allowed: see PUSH RULES below for the details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:124
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--all`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--branches`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:128
msgid ""
"Push all branches (i.e. refs under `refs/heads/`); cannot be used with other "
"<refspec>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:129
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--prune`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:136
msgid ""
"Remove remote branches that don't have a local counterpart. For example a "
"remote branch `tmp` will be removed if a local branch with the same name "
"doesn't exist any more. This also respects refspecs, e.g.  `git push --prune "
"remote refs/heads/*:refs/tmp/*` would make sure that remote `refs/tmp/foo` "
"will be removed if `refs/heads/foo` doesn't exist."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:137
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--mirror`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:147
msgid ""
"Instead of naming each ref to push, specifies that all refs under `refs/` "
"(which includes but is not limited to `refs/heads/`, `refs/remotes/`, and "
"`refs/tags/`)  be mirrored to the remote repository.  Newly created local "
"refs will be pushed to the remote end, locally updated refs will be force "
"updated on the remote end, and deleted refs will be removed from the remote "
"end.  This is the default if the configuration option "
"`remote.<remote>.mirror` is set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--dry-run`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:151
msgid "Do everything except actually send the updates."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:152
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--porcelain`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:156
msgid ""
"Produce machine-readable output.  The output status line for each ref will "
"be tab-separated and sent to stdout instead of stderr.  The full symbolic "
"names of the refs will be given."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-d`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--delete`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:161
msgid ""
"All listed refs are deleted from the remote repository. This is the same as "
"prefixing all refs with a colon."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--tags`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:166
msgid ""
"All refs under `refs/tags` are pushed, in addition to refspecs explicitly "
"listed on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:167
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--follow-tags`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:174
msgid ""
"Push all the refs that would be pushed without this option, and also push "
"annotated tags in `refs/tags` that are missing from the remote but are "
"pointing at commit-ish that are reachable from the refs being pushed.  This "
"can also be specified with configuration variable `push.followTags`.  For "
"more information, see `push.followTags` in linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:175
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--signed`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-signed`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--signed=(true|false|if-asked)`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:186
msgid ""
"GPG-sign the push request to update refs on the receiving side, to allow it "
"to be checked by the hooks and/or be logged.  If `false` or `--no-signed`, "
"no signing will be attempted.  If `true` or `--signed`, the push will fail "
"if the server does not support signed pushes.  If set to `if-asked`, sign if "
"and only if the server supports signed pushes.  The push will also fail if "
"the actual call to `gpg --sign` fails.  See linkgit:git-receive-pack[1] for "
"the details on the receiving end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:187
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--atomic`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-atomic`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:192
msgid ""
"Use an atomic transaction on the remote side if available.  Either all refs "
"are updated, or on error, no refs are updated.  If the server does not "
"support atomic pushes the push will fail."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-o <option>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:194
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--push-option=<option>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:204
msgid ""
"Transmit the given string to the server, which passes them to the "
"pre-receive as well as the post-receive hook. The given string must not "
"contain a NUL or LF character.  When multiple `--push-option=<option>` are "
"given, they are all sent to the other side in the order listed on the "
"command line.  When no `--push-option=<option>` is given from the command "
"line, the values of configuration variable `push.pushOption` are used "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:205
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--receive-pack=<git-receive-pack>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:206
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--exec=<git-receive-pack>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:211
msgid ""
"Path to the 'git-receive-pack' program on the remote end.  Sometimes useful "
"when pushing to a remote repository over ssh, and you do not have the "
"program in a directory on the default $PATH."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:212
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force-with-lease`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:213
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-force-with-lease`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:214
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force-with-lease=<refname>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:215
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force-with-lease=<refname>:<expect>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:218
msgid ""
"Usually, \"git push\" refuses to update a remote ref that is not an ancestor "
"of the local ref used to overwrite it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:221
msgid ""
"This option overrides this restriction if the current value of the remote "
"ref is the expected value.  \"git push\" fails otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:228
msgid ""
"Imagine that you have to rebase what you have already published.  You will "
"have to bypass the \"must fast-forward\" rule in order to replace the "
"history you originally published with the rebased history.  If somebody else "
"built on top of your original history while you are rebasing, the tip of the "
"branch at the remote may advance with their commit, and blindly pushing with "
"`--force` will lose their work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:235
msgid ""
"This option allows you to say that you expect the history you are updating "
"is what you rebased and want to replace. If the remote ref still points at "
"the commit you specified, you can be sure that no other people did anything "
"to the ref. It is like taking a \"lease\" on the ref without explicitly "
"locking it, and the remote ref is updated only if the \"lease\" is still "
"valid."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:240
msgid ""
"`--force-with-lease` alone, without specifying the details, will protect all "
"remote refs that are going to be updated by requiring their current value to "
"be the same as the remote-tracking branch we have for them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:245
msgid ""
"`--force-with-lease=<refname>`, without specifying the expected value, will "
"protect the named ref (alone), if it is going to be updated, by requiring "
"its current value to be the same as the remote-tracking branch we have for "
"it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:253
msgid ""
"`--force-with-lease=<refname>:<expect>` will protect the named ref (alone), "
"if it is going to be updated, by requiring its current value to be the same "
"as the specified value `<expect>` (which is allowed to be different from the "
"remote-tracking branch we have for the refname, or we do not even have to "
"have such a remote-tracking branch when this form is used).  If `<expect>` "
"is the empty string, then the named ref must not already exist."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:258
msgid ""
"Note that all forms other than `--force-with-lease=<refname>:<expect>` that "
"specifies the expected current value of the ref explicitly are still "
"experimental and their semantics may change as we gain experience with this "
"feature."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:261
msgid ""
"\"--no-force-with-lease\" will cancel all the previous --force-with-lease on "
"the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:267
msgid ""
"A general note on safety: supplying this option without an expected value, "
"i.e. as `--force-with-lease` or `--force-with-lease=<refname>` interacts "
"very badly with anything that implicitly runs `git fetch` on the remote to "
"be pushed to in the background, e.g. `git fetch origin` on your repository "
"in a cronjob."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:274
msgid ""
"The protection it offers over `--force` is ensuring that subsequent changes "
"your work wasn't based on aren't clobbered, but this is trivially defeated "
"if some background process is updating refs in the background. We don't have "
"anything except the remote tracking info to go by as a heuristic for refs "
"you're expected to have seen & are willing to clobber."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:278
msgid ""
"If your editor or some other system is running `git fetch` in the background "
"for you a way to mitigate this is to simply set up another remote:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:281
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit remote add origin-push $(git config remote.origin.url)\n"
"\tgit fetch origin-push\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:284
msgid ""
"Now when the background process runs `git fetch origin` the references on "
"`origin-push` won't be updated, and thus commands like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:286
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tgit push --force-with-lease origin-push\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:291
msgid ""
"Will fail unless you manually run `git fetch origin-push`. This method is of "
"course entirely defeated by something that runs `git fetch --all`, in that "
"case you'd need to either disable it or do something more tedious like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:296
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit fetch              # update 'master' from remote\n"
"\tgit tag base master    # mark our base point\n"
"\tgit rebase -i master   # rewrite some commits\n"
"\tgit push --force-with-lease=master:base master:master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:302
msgid ""
"I.e. create a `base` tag for versions of the upstream code that you've seen "
"and are willing to overwrite, then rewrite history, and finally force push "
"changes to `master` if the remote version is still at `base`, regardless of "
"what your local `remotes/origin/master` has been updated to in the "
"background."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:310
msgid ""
"Alternatively, specifying `--force-if-includes` as an ancillary option along "
"with `--force-with-lease[=<refname>]` (i.e., without saying what exact "
"commit the ref on the remote side must be pointing at, or which refs on the "
"remote side are being protected) at the time of \"push\" will verify if "
"updates from the remote-tracking refs that may have been implicitly updated "
"in the background are integrated locally before allowing a forced update."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:311
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-f`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:312
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:315
msgid ""
"Usually, `git push` will refuse to update a branch that is not an ancestor "
"of the commit being pushed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:319
msgid ""
"This flag disables that check, the other safety checks in PUSH RULES below, "
"and the checks in --force-with-lease. It can cause the remote repository to "
"lose commits; use it with care."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:328
msgid ""
"Note that `--force` applies to all the refs that are pushed, hence using it "
"with `push.default` set to `matching` or with multiple push destinations "
"configured with `remote.*.push` may overwrite refs other than the current "
"branch (including local refs that are strictly behind their remote "
"counterpart).  To force a push to only one branch, use a `+` in front of the "
"refspec to push (e.g `git push origin +master` to force a push to the "
"`master` branch). See the `<refspec>...` section above for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:329
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force-if-includes`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:330
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-force-if-includes`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:333
msgid ""
"Force an update only if the tip of the remote-tracking ref has been "
"integrated locally."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:339
msgid ""
"This option enables a check that verifies if the tip of the remote-tracking "
"ref is reachable from one of the \"reflog\" entries of the local branch "
"based in it for a rewrite. The check ensures that any updates from the "
"remote have been incorporated locally by rejecting the forced update if that "
"is not the case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:343
msgid ""
"If the option is passed without specifying `--force-with-lease`, or "
"specified along with `--force-with-lease=<refname>:<expect>`, it is a "
"\"no-op\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:345
msgid "Specifying `--no-force-if-includes` disables this behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:346
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--repo=<repository>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:349
msgid ""
"This option is equivalent to the <repository> argument. If both are "
"specified, the command-line argument takes precedence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:350
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-u`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:351
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--set-upstream`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:356
msgid ""
"For every branch that is up to date or successfully pushed, add upstream "
"(tracking) reference, used by argument-less linkgit:git-pull[1] and other "
"commands. For more information, see `branch.<name>.merge` in "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:357
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--thin`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:358
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-thin`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:363
msgid ""
"These options are passed to linkgit:git-send-pack[1]. A thin transfer "
"significantly reduces the amount of sent data when the sender and receiver "
"share many of the same objects in common. The default is `--thin`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:364
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:365
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:369
msgid ""
"Suppress all output, including the listing of updated refs, unless an error "
"occurs. Progress is not reported to the standard error stream."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:370
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-v`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:371
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verbose`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:373
msgid "Run verbosely."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--progress`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:379
msgid ""
"Progress status is reported on the standard error stream by default when it "
"is attached to a terminal, unless -q is specified. This flag forces progress "
"status even if the standard error stream is not directed to a terminal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:380
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-recurse-submodules`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:381
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--recurse-submodules=check|on-demand|only|no`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:395
msgid ""
"May be used to make sure all submodule commits used by the revisions to be "
"pushed are available on a remote-tracking branch.  If 'check' is used Git "
"will verify that all submodule commits that changed in the revisions to be "
"pushed are available on at least one remote of the submodule. If any commits "
"are missing the push will be aborted and exit with non-zero status. If "
"'on-demand' is used all submodules that changed in the revisions to be "
"pushed will be pushed. If on-demand was not able to push all necessary "
"revisions it will also be aborted and exit with non-zero status. If 'only' "
"is used all submodules will be pushed while the superproject is left "
"unpushed. A value of 'no' or using `--no-recurse-submodules` can be used to "
"override the push.recurseSubmodules configuration variable when no submodule "
"recursion is required."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:399
msgid ""
"When using 'on-demand' or 'only', if a submodule has a "
"\"push.recurseSubmodules={on-demand,only}\" or \"submodule.recurse\" "
"configuration, further recursion will occur. In this case, \"only\" is "
"treated as \"on-demand\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:400
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verify`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:401
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-verify`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:405
msgid ""
"Toggle the pre-push hook (see linkgit:githooks[5]).  The default is "
"--verify, giving the hook a chance to prevent the push.  With --no-verify, "
"the hook is bypassed completely."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:406
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-4`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:407
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ipv4`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:409
msgid "Use IPv4 addresses only, ignoring IPv6 addresses."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:410
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-6`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:411
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ipv6`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:413
msgid "Use IPv6 addresses only, ignoring IPv4 addresses."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:417
#, no-wrap
msgid "OUTPUT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:422
msgid ""
"The output of \"git push\" depends on the transport method used; this "
"section describes the output when pushing over the Git protocol (either "
"locally or via ssh)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:425
msgid ""
"The status of the push is output in tabular form, with each line "
"representing the status of a single ref. Each line is of the form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:428
#, no-wrap
msgid " <flag> <summary> <from> -> <to> (<reason>)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:431
msgid "If --porcelain is used, then each line of the output is of the form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:434
#, no-wrap
msgid " <flag> \\t <from>:<to> \\t <summary> (<reason>)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:438
msgid ""
"The status of up-to-date refs is shown only if --porcelain or --verbose "
"option is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:439
#, no-wrap
msgid "flag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:441
msgid "A single character indicating the status of the ref:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:441
#, no-wrap
msgid "(space)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:442
msgid "for a successfully pushed fast-forward;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:442
#, no-wrap
msgid "`+`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:443
msgid "for a successful forced update;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:443
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:444
msgid "for a successfully deleted ref;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:444
#, no-wrap
msgid "`*`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:445
msgid "for a successfully pushed new ref;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:445
#, no-wrap
msgid "`!`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:446
msgid "for a ref that was rejected or failed to push; and"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:446
#, no-wrap
msgid "`=`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:447
msgid "for a ref that was up to date and did not need pushing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:448
#, no-wrap
msgid "summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:453
msgid ""
"For a successfully pushed ref, the summary shows the old and new values of "
"the ref in a form suitable for using as an argument to `git log` (this is "
"`<old>..<new>` in most cases, and `<old>...<new>` for forced "
"non-fast-forward updates)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:455
msgid "For a failed update, more details are given:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:457
#, no-wrap
msgid "rejected"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:460
msgid ""
"Git did not try to send the ref at all, typically because it is not a "
"fast-forward and you did not force the update."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:461
#, no-wrap
msgid "remote rejected"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:469
msgid ""
"The remote end refused the update.  Usually caused by a hook on the remote "
"side, or because the remote repository has one of the following safety "
"options in effect: `receive.denyCurrentBranch` (for pushes to the checked "
"out branch), `receive.denyNonFastForwards` (for forced non-fast-forward "
"updates), `receive.denyDeletes` or `receive.denyDeleteCurrent`.  See "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:470
#, no-wrap
msgid "remote failure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:474
msgid ""
"The remote end did not report the successful update of the ref, perhaps "
"because of a temporary error on the remote side, a break in the network "
"connection, or other transient error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:476
#, no-wrap
msgid "from"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:480
msgid ""
"The name of the local ref being pushed, minus its `refs/<type>/` prefix. In "
"the case of deletion, the name of the local ref is omitted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:481
#, no-wrap
msgid "to"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:484
msgid "The name of the remote ref being updated, minus its `refs/<type>/` prefix."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:485
#, no-wrap
msgid "reason"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:489
msgid ""
"A human-readable explanation. In the case of successfully pushed refs, no "
"explanation is needed. For a failed ref, the reason for failure is "
"described."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:491
#, no-wrap
msgid "PUSH RULES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:495
msgid ""
"As a safety feature, the `git push` command only allows certain kinds of "
"updates to prevent you from accidentally losing data on the remote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:501
msgid ""
"Because branches and tags are intended to be used differently, the safety "
"rules for pushing to a branch are different from the rules for pushing to a "
"tag. In the following rules \"update\" means any modifications except "
"deletions and creations. Deletions and creations are always allowed, except "
"when forbidden by configuration or hooks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:505
msgid ""
"If the push destination is a **branch** (`refs/heads/*`): only fast-forward "
"updates are allowed, which means the destination must be an ancestor of the "
"source commit. The source must be a commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:507
msgid ""
"If the push destination is a **tag** (`refs/tags/*`): all updates will be "
"rejected. The source can be any object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:508
msgid "If the push destination is not a branch or tag:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:509
msgid "If the source is a tree or blob object, any updates will be rejected"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:517
msgid ""
"If the source is a tag or commit object, any fast-forward update is allowed, "
"even in cases where what's being fast-forwarded is not a commit, but a tag "
"object which happens to point to a new commit which is a fast-forward of the "
"commit the last tag (or commit) it's replacing. Replacing a tag with an "
"entirely different tag is also allowed, if it points to the same commit, as "
"well as pushing a peeled tag, i.e. pushing the commit that existing tag "
"object points to, or a new tag object which an existing commit points to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:523
msgid ""
"You can override these rules by passing `--force` or by adding the optional "
"leading `+` to a refspec. The only exceptions are that no amount of forcing "
"will make a branch accept a non-commit object, and forcing won't make the "
"remote repository accept a push that it's configured to deny."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:528
msgid ""
"Hooks and configuration can also override or amend these rules, see "
"e.g. `receive.denyNonFastForwards` and `receive.denyDeletes` in "
"linkgit:git-config[1] and `pre-receive` and `update` in linkgit:githooks[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:530
#, no-wrap
msgid "NOTE ABOUT FAST-FORWARDS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:535
msgid ""
"When an update changes a branch (or more in general, a ref) that used to "
"point at commit A to point at another commit B, it is called a fast-forward "
"update if and only if B is a descendant of A."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:539
msgid ""
"In a fast-forward update from A to B, the set of commits that the original "
"commit A built on top of is a subset of the commits the new commit B builds "
"on top of.  Hence, it does not lose any history."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:544
msgid ""
"In contrast, a non-fast-forward update will lose history.  For example, "
"suppose you and somebody else started at the same commit X, and you built a "
"history leading to commit B while the other person built a history leading "
"to commit A.  The history looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      B\n"
"     /\n"
" ---X---A\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:556
msgid ""
"Further suppose that the other person already pushed changes leading to A "
"back to the original repository from which you two obtained the original "
"commit X."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:559
msgid ""
"The push done by the other person updated the branch that used to point at "
"commit X to point at commit A.  It is a fast-forward."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:564
msgid ""
"But if you try to push, you will attempt to update the branch (that now "
"points at A) with commit B.  This does _not_ fast-forward.  If you did so, "
"the changes introduced by commit A will be lost, because everybody will now "
"start building on top of B."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:567
msgid ""
"The command by default does not allow an update that is not a fast-forward "
"to prevent such loss of history."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:572
msgid ""
"If you do not want to lose your work (history from X to B) or the work by "
"the other person (history from X to A), you would need to first fetch the "
"history from the repository, create a history that contains changes done by "
"both parties, and push the result back."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:576
msgid ""
"You can perform \"git pull\", resolve potential conflicts, and \"git push\" "
"the result.  A \"git pull\" will create a merge commit C between commits A "
"and B."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:582
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      B---C\n"
"     /   /\n"
" ---X---A\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:587
msgid ""
"Updating A with the resulting merge commit will fast-forward and your push "
"will be accepted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:592
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can rebase your change between X and B on top of A, with "
"\"git pull --rebase\", and push the result back.  The rebase will create a "
"new commit D that builds the change between X and B on top of A."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:598
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      B   D\n"
"     /   /\n"
" ---X---A\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:603
msgid ""
"Again, updating A with this commit will fast-forward and your push will be "
"accepted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:614
msgid ""
"There is another common situation where you may encounter non-fast-forward "
"rejection when you try to push, and it is possible even when you are pushing "
"into a repository nobody else pushes into. After you push commit A yourself "
"(in the first picture in this section), replace it with \"git commit "
"--amend\" to produce commit B, and you try to push it out, because forgot "
"that you have pushed A out already. In such a case, and only if you are "
"certain that nobody in the meantime fetched your earlier commit A (and "
"started building on top of it), you can run \"git push --force\" to "
"overwrite it. In other words, \"git push --force\" is a method reserved for "
"a case where you do mean to lose history."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:617
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:619
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:623
msgid ""
"Works like `git push <remote>`, where <remote> is the current branch's "
"remote (or `origin`, if no remote is configured for the current branch)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:624
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:629
msgid ""
"Without additional configuration, pushes the current branch to the "
"configured upstream (`branch.<name>.merge` configuration variable) if it has "
"the same name as the current branch, and errors out without pushing "
"otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:633
msgid ""
"The default behavior of this command when no <refspec> is given can be "
"configured by setting the `push` option of the remote, or the `push.default` "
"configuration variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:638
msgid ""
"For example, to default to pushing only the current branch to `origin` use "
"`git config remote.origin.push HEAD`.  Any valid <refspec> (like the ones in "
"the examples below) can be configured as the default for `git push origin`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:639
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin :`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:643
msgid ""
"Push \"matching\" branches to `origin`. See <refspec> in the "
"<<OPTIONS,OPTIONS>> section above for a description of \"matching\" "
"branches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:644
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin master`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:650
msgid ""
"Find a ref that matches `master` in the source repository (most likely, it "
"would find `refs/heads/master`), and update the same ref "
"(e.g. `refs/heads/master`) in `origin` repository with it.  If `master` did "
"not exist remotely, it would be created."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:651
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin HEAD`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:654
msgid "A handy way to push the current branch to the same name on the remote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:655
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push mothership master:satellite/master dev:satellite/dev`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:660
msgid ""
"Use the source ref that matches `master` (e.g. `refs/heads/master`)  to "
"update the ref that matches `satellite/master` (most probably "
"`refs/remotes/satellite/master`) in the `mothership` repository; do the same "
"for `dev` and `satellite/dev`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:663
msgid ""
"See the section describing `<refspec>...` above for a discussion of the "
"matching semantics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:670
msgid ""
"This is to emulate `git fetch` run on the `mothership` using `git push` that "
"is run in the opposite direction in order to integrate the work done on "
"`satellite`, and is often necessary when you can only make connection in one "
"way (i.e. satellite can ssh into mothership but mothership cannot initiate "
"connection to satellite because the latter is behind a firewall or does not "
"run sshd)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:675
msgid ""
"After running this `git push` on the `satellite` machine, you would ssh into "
"the `mothership` and run `git merge` there to complete the emulation of `git "
"pull` that were run on `mothership` to pull changes made on `satellite`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:676
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin HEAD:master`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:680
msgid ""
"Push the current branch to the remote ref matching `master` in the `origin` "
"repository. This form is convenient to push the current branch without "
"thinking about its local name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:681
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin master:refs/heads/experimental`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:687
msgid ""
"Create the branch `experimental` in the `origin` repository by copying the "
"current `master` branch.  This form is only needed to create a new branch or "
"tag in the remote repository when the local name and the remote name are "
"different; otherwise, the ref name on its own will work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:688
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin :experimental`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:691
msgid ""
"Find a ref that matches `experimental` in the `origin` repository "
"(e.g. `refs/heads/experimental`), and delete it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:692
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git push origin +dev:master`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:697
msgid ""
"Update the origin repository's master branch with the dev branch, allowing "
"non-fast-forward updates.  *This can leave unreferenced commits dangling in "
"the origin repository.* Consider the following situation, where a "
"fast-forward is not possible:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:702
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t    o---o---o---A---B  origin/master\n"
"\t\t     \\\n"
"\t\t      X---Y---Z  dev\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:705
msgid "The above command would change the origin repository to"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:710
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t      A---B  (unnamed branch)\n"
"\t\t     /\n"
"\t    o---o---o---X---Y---Z  master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-push.adoc:715
msgid ""
"Commits A and B would no longer belong to a branch with a symbolic name, and "
"so would be unreachable.  As such, these commits would be removed by a `git "
"gc` command on the origin repository."
msgstr ""

